<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>lenses</title></head><body><h1>lenses</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Lens Maker's Formula</li><li>Spherical Lenses and Angular Magnification</li><li>Spherical Lenses, Angular Magnification</li><li>Lens Maker's Formula</li><li>Power of a Lens</li><li>Power of a Lens</li></ul><h2>Lens Maker's Formula</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The lens-maker relation predicts focal length from material and curvature. For a thin lens in air, 1/f=(n−1)(1/R1−1/R2), where n is lens refractive index and R1,R2 are signed radii in a stated convention.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For light travelling left to right, a biconvex lens commonly has R1>0 and R2<0, so the curvature contributions add to positive converging power. Do not substitute unsigned radii into a signed formula.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Worked example: n=1.50, R1=+20 cm and R2=−20 cm give 1/f=0.50(1/20+1/20)=0.05 cm⁻¹, hence f=+20 cm.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>In a surrounding medium use relative index nrel=nlens/nmedium. As nrel approaches 1, the lens bends light less and |f| increases; a lens can change sign if the surrounding medium has a larger index.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The formula assumes a thin lens and paraxial rays. Thick lenses require principal-plane treatment, and very wide ray bundles show aberrations.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Focal length depends on curvature and relative refractive index.</td></tr><tr><td>A consistent sign convention is essential.</td></tr><tr><td>The thin-lens formula neglects centre thickness.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The formula assumes a thin lens and paraxial rays.</td></tr><tr><td>Do not replace signed radii by their magnitudes.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The lens-maker formula links curvature and relative refractive index to focal length. Signed radii and the surrounding medium determine the correct optical power.</p><hr><h2>Spherical Lenses and Angular Magnification</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A spherical lens has two refracting surfaces that are portions of spheres. A convex lens is usually converging in air; a concave lens is usually diverging. Immersion in a medium with a different refractive index can alter this behaviour.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Linear magnification m=hi/ho=v/u in the Cartesian lens convention compares image and object heights. Its magnitude gives size ratio; its sign indicates orientation.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Angular magnification M=β/α compares the angle subtended at the eye with the instrument to that without it. For a simple magnifier with near point D, M≈1+D/f; for relaxed viewing at infinity, M≈D/f.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Worked example: a magnifier with f=5.0 cm used at the conventional near point D=25 cm gives M≈1+25/5=6. A shorter focal length gives greater angular magnification.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The near point is a convention (often 25 cm for a normal eye). Real comfortable viewing distance varies; a magnifier also has a limited field of view and depth of focus.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Linear magnification compares sizes; angular magnification compares visual angles.</td></tr><tr><td>Use consistent units for D and f.</td></tr><tr><td>The stated near point is conventional, not universal.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Near-point formulas assume an ideal thin magnifier and paraxial rays.</td></tr><tr><td>Do not use centimetres for one distance and metres for the other.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A lens magnifier increases the visual angle of a nearby object. Distinguish angular magnification from image-size magnification, and state the viewing convention used.</p><hr><h2>Spherical Lenses, Angular Magnification</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Spherical Lens: A lens is a transparent medium bounded by two spherical surfaces (or one plane and one spherical). Convex lens (converging) is thicker at center; concave lens (diverging) is thinner at center.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Lens Maker's Formula: $\frac{1}{f} = (\mu - 1)\left(\frac{1}{R_1} - \frac{1}{R_2}\right)$ where $\mu$ is refractive index of lens material relative to medium, $R_1$ and $R_2$ are radii of curvature (Cartesian sign convention).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Lens Formula: $\frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f}$ using sign convention (u negative for object on left). Magnification $m = v/u = h_i/h_o$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Power of Lens: $P = 1/f$ (f in metres). Unit: dioptre (D). Convex lens has positive power; concave has negative power. Lenses in contact: $P = P_1 + P_2$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Combination of Lenses: Two lenses separated by distance d: equivalent focal length $\frac{1}{F} = \frac{1}{f_1} + \frac{1}{f_2} - \frac{d}{f_1 f_2}$. Power: $P = P_1 + P_2 - d P_1 P_2$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Angular Magnification: For a simple magnifier, angular magnification $M = \theta_i/\theta_o$. When image at near point: $M = 1 + D/f$; when image at infinity: $M = D/f$, where $D = 25\text{ cm}$ (least distance of distinct vision).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Compound Microscope: Total magnification $M = m_o \times m_e = (v_o/u_o)(1 + D/f_e)$ (final image at near point) or $(v_o/u_o)(D/f_e)$ (at infinity).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Astronomical Telescope: Angular magnification $M = f_o/f_e$ (normal adjustment, final at infinity) or $M = (f_o/f_e)(1 + f_e/D)$ (final at near point). Length $L = f_o + f_e$.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Convex lens converges; concave lens diverges.</td></tr><tr><td>Lens maker's formula relates f to μ, R₁, R₂.</td></tr><tr><td>Power P = 1/f in dioptres; positive for convex, negative for concave.</td></tr><tr><td>Angular magnification: M = 1 + D/f (near point), M = D/f (infinity).</td></tr><tr><td>Telescope magnification: M = fₒ/fₑ.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Confusing lens formula sign: 1/v - 1/u = 1/f vs mirror formula 1/v + 1/u = 1/f.</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking magnification m = v/u applies to angular magnification: Linear m = v/u, angular M = θ'/θ — different concepts.</td></tr><tr><td>Believing power adds for separated lenses: Powers add only for lenses in contact (d = 0).</td></tr><tr><td>Using D = 25 cm for all eyes: D varies with age and individual (near point changes).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Spherical lenses converge (convex) or diverge (concave) light. Lens maker's formula gives f from μ, R₁, R₂. Power P = 1/f in dioptres. Angular magnification for magnifier: M = 1 + D/f (near point) or D/f (infinity). Telescope: M = fₒ/fₑ.</p><hr><h2>Lens Maker's Formula</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>The lens-maker relation predicts focal length from material and curvature. For a thin lens in air, 1/f=(n−1)(1/R1−1/R2), where n is lens refractive index and R1,R2 are signed radii in a stated convention.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>For light travelling left to right, a biconvex lens commonly has R1>0 and R2<0, so the curvature contributions add to positive converging power. Do not substitute unsigned radii into a signed formula.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Worked example: n=1.50, R1=+20 cm and R2=−20 cm give 1/f=0.50(1/20+1/20)=0.05 cm⁻¹, hence f=+20 cm.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>In a surrounding medium use relative index nrel=nlens/nmedium. As nrel approaches 1, the lens bends light less and |f| increases; a lens can change sign if the surrounding medium has a larger index.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>The formula assumes a thin lens and paraxial rays. Thick lenses require principal-plane treatment, and very wide ray bundles show aberrations.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Focal length depends on curvature and relative refractive index.</td></tr><tr><td>A consistent sign convention is essential.</td></tr><tr><td>The thin-lens formula neglects centre thickness.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The formula assumes a thin lens and paraxial rays.</td></tr><tr><td>Do not replace signed radii by their magnitudes.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The lens-maker formula links curvature and relative refractive index to focal length. Signed radii and the surrounding medium determine the correct optical power.</p><hr><h2>Power of a Lens</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Lens power measures convergence or divergence: P=1/f when f is in metres. Its SI-derived unit is the dioptre, 1 D=1 m⁻¹. A converging lens has positive power and a diverging lens negative power in air.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Worked example: f=+25 cm=+0.25 m gives P=+4 D. A diverging lens with f=−50 cm=−0.50 m has P=−2 D.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Thin lenses in contact have additive powers. A +4 D lens and −1.5 D lens make Peq=+2.5 D, so feq=1/2.5=0.40 m.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Power is not magnification: focal length determines power, while image size and orientation also depend on object distance through the lens equation.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>For separated lenses the equivalent power includes separation; the simple algebraic sum applies only when thin lenses are in contact or separation is negligible.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Convert focal length to metres before calculating dioptres.</td></tr><tr><td>Add powers algebraically only for thin lenses in contact.</td></tr><tr><td>The sign distinguishes convergence from divergence.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Using centimetres directly produces a power 100 times too large.</td></tr><tr><td>Separated or thick lens systems require an additional relation.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Lens power is signed reciprocal focal length in metres. Contact lens powers add, but magnification still depends on object position.</p><hr><h2>Power of a Lens</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Lens power measures convergence or divergence: P=1/f when f is in metres. Its SI-derived unit is the dioptre, 1 D=1 m⁻¹. A converging lens has positive power and a diverging lens negative power in air.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Worked example: f=+25 cm=+0.25 m gives P=+4 D. A diverging lens with f=−50 cm=−0.50 m has P=−2 D.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Thin lenses in contact have additive powers. A +4 D lens and −1.5 D lens make Peq=+2.5 D, so feq=1/2.5=0.40 m.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Power is not magnification: focal length determines power, while image size and orientation also depend on object distance through the lens equation.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>For separated lenses the equivalent power includes separation; the simple algebraic sum applies only when thin lenses are in contact or separation is negligible.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Convert focal length to metres before calculating dioptres.</td></tr><tr><td>Add powers algebraically only for thin lenses in contact.</td></tr><tr><td>The sign distinguishes convergence from divergence.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Using centimetres directly produces a power 100 times too large.</td></tr><tr><td>Separated or thick lens systems require an additional relation.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Lens power is signed reciprocal focal length in metres. Contact lens powers add, but magnification still depends on object position.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Lens Maker's Formula</td><td>The lens-maker formula links curvature and relative refractive index to focal length. Signed radii and the surrounding medium determine the correct optical power.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Spherical Lenses and Angular Magnification</td><td>A lens magnifier increases the visual angle of a nearby object. Distinguish angular magnification from image-size magnification, and state the viewing convention used.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Spherical Lenses, Angular Magnification</td><td>Spherical lenses converge (convex) or diverge (concave) light. Lens maker's formula gives f from μ, R₁, R₂. Power P = 1/f in dioptres. Angular magnification for magnifier: M = 1 + D/f (near point) or D/f (infinity). Telescope: M = fₒ/fₑ.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Lens Maker's Formula</td><td>The lens-maker formula links curvature and relative refractive index to focal length. Signed radii and the surrounding medium determine the correct optical power.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Power of a Lens</td><td>Lens power is signed reciprocal focal length in metres. Contact lens powers add, but magnification still depends on object position.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Power of a Lens</td><td>Lens power is signed reciprocal focal length in metres. Contact lens powers add, but magnification still depends on object position.</td></tr></table></body></html>